(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the 'High-Impact Technical Design Case Study' format for computer systems engineering portfolios. Outline the six mandatory sections that prove senior engineering competence.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Purpose of the Technical Design Case Study:) Tj
T*
(   - Elevates an engineering portfolio above superficial code repositories, demonstrating systemic) Tj
T*
(   reasoning, architectural trade-off balance, and business impact to engineering directors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Six Mandatory Architectural Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - Section 1: Executive Summary & Problem Context:) Tj
T*
(   * High-level summary of the domain, the human stakes, and the quantified business challenge) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., 'Sashing API timeout latency during peak holiday traffic'\).) Tj
T*
(   - Section 2: User Research & Systems Constraints:) Tj
T*
(   * Articulates user pain points and technical constraints \(e.g., memory budgets, bandwidth) Tj
T*
(   limits, legacy database incompatibilities, regulatory compliance\).) Tj
T*
(   - Section 3: Evaluated Architectural Alternatives & Trade-Off Matrix:) Tj
T*
(   * Explains the alternative paradigms considered \(e.g., REST vs. GraphQL vs. gRPC; SQL vs.) Tj
T*
(   NoSQL\), detailing why alternatives were rejected using a weighted trade-off matrix.) Tj
T*
(   - Section 4: System Architecture & Data Flow:) Tj
T*
(   * Detailed architectural block diagrams, state machine topologies, and sequence flows) Tj
T*
(   illustrating component interactions, data persistence, and caching strategies.) Tj
T*
(   - Section 5: Verification, Testing, & Usability Validation:) Tj
T*
(   * Empirical proof of success: unit and integration test coverage, chaos stress-testing) Tj
T*
(   benchmarks, and human usability testing metrics \(SUS score, Task Completion Rate\).) Tj
T*
(   - Section 6: Business Impact, Lessons Learned, & Future Roadmap:) Tj
T*
(   * Quantified business outcomes \(e.g., 'Reduced cloud hosting OpEx by $340k/yr, achieved 99.99%) Tj
T*
(   uptime'\), honest reflections on technical debt accepted, and next-phase architectural) Tj
T*
(   iterations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 18:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze how 'The Peak-End Rule' and 'Zeigarnik Effect' must be considered during the final project demonstration and presentation delivery.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Psychological Cognitive Heuristics in Presentations:) Tj
T*
(   - Peak-End Rule \(Daniel Kahneman\): Human memory does not evaluate an experience by averaging) Tj
T*
(   every minute. Humans remember an experience almost exclusively by its emotional PEAK \(the most) Tj
T*
(   intense moment\) and its final END.) Tj
T*
(   - Zeigarnik Effect \(Bluma Zeigarnik\): Unfinished, unresolved tasks occupy cognitive working) Tj
T*
(   memory and create tension; once an open loop is closed, the mind relaxes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Engineering Presentation Delivery Strategy:) Tj
T*
(   - Engineering the Peak \(The 'Aha!' Demonstration\):) Tj
T*
(   * Do not bury the live product demo in slide 25 after 40 minutes of dry bullet points!) Tj
T*
(   * Place the high-impact, dramatic product demonstration at the 15-minute mark \(the peak\). Show) Tj
T*
(   the system performing an astonishing feat: auto-healing a simulated cluster failure in 3) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
