/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the ethical and technical challenges of 'AI Model Transparency' during intelligent product demonstrations. How does an engineering team present probabilistic systems without deceptive over-promising?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Probabilistic AI Demonstration Trap:) Tj
T*
(   - Traditional software is deterministic \(code either works or throws an error\); Machine) Tj
T*
(   Learning models are probabilistic \(they generate inferences with variable statistical) Tj
T*
(   confidence and inevitable hallucinations\).) Tj
T*
(   - Demonstrating AI products using carefully staged, cherry-picked 'canned' demos deceives) Tj
T*
(   stakeholders, setting unrealistic expectations that lead to catastrophic backlash when deployed) Tj
T*
(   in production.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Four Protocols for Ethical AI Product Demonstrations:) Tj
T*
(   - Protocol 1: Expose Confidence Scores & Uncertainty:) Tj
T*
(   * In the interface, explicitly render the model's prediction confidence alongside the output:) Tj
T*
(   'Model Confidence: 78% \(Moderate\). Suggested action requires human verification.') Tj
T*
(   - Protocol 2: Demonstrate Edge Cases and Failure Recovery:) Tj
T*
(   * Deliberately show what happens when the model encounters ambiguous input, showing how the) Tj
T*
(   interface smoothly gracefully degrades into human-in-the-loop review.) Tj
T*
(   - Protocol 3: Real-Time Live Inference:) Tj
T*
(   * Avoid pre-recorded video animations; run live inference queries submitted spontaneously by) Tj
T*
(   the audience to demonstrate genuine capability.) Tj
T*
(   - Protocol 4: Transparent Architectural Explainability:) Tj
T*
(   * Explain the model's training boundaries, known biases, and compute cost per inference,) Tj
T*
(   proving the team understands real-world operational economics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail how 'Net Revenue Retention' \(NRR\) and 'Customer Cohort Churn' validate the long-term success of an enterprise software redesign.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Beyond Superficial Activation Metrics:) Tj
T*
(   - A flashy UI redesign often produces an immediate spike in initial page views and signups) Tj
T*
(   driven by marketing campaigns. However, if the redesigned interaction flows induce long-term) Tj
T*
(   cognitive fatigue, customers will silently churn after 90 days.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Decisive Financial & Behavioral Health Metrics:) Tj
T*
(   - Metric 1: Net Revenue Retention \(NRR\):) Tj
T*
(   * Formula: \(Starting MRR + Expansion MRR - Contraction MRR - Churn MRR\) / Starting MRR * 100.) Tj
T*
(   * An NRR > 120% proves that existing enterprise customers are not only staying, but expanding) Tj
T*
(   their usage and seat licenses over time because the redesigned platform delivers compounding) Tj
T*
(   operational ROI.) Tj
T*
(   - Metric 2: Asymptotic Cohort Retention Curves:) Tj
T*
(   * Plotting user retention percentages across successive weekly cohorts. If the curve continues) Tj
T*
(   plunging toward zero, the redesign failed. If the curve flattens out into a horizontal plateau) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., retaining 45% of users indefinitely\), the redesign achieved structural product-market) Tj
T*
(   fit.) Tj
T*
(   - Metric 3: Task Time and Error Rate Decay:) Tj
T*
